-- title:
--   Odd Vélu step equals Vélu quotient with image subgroup
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field, let $E$ be a Weierstrass curve over $L$, let $\ell$ be a prime with $\ell \neq 2$, let $H$ be a finite cyclic additive subgroup of the group of points of the affine curve $E$, and let $Q \in H$ satisfy $\operatorname{addOrderOf} Q = \ell$. Write $S = E.\mathtt{oddOrderSummingSet}\,Q\,(\ell/2)$ for the finite set of pairs $(x,y)$ obtained as `coordsOrZero` of $k \cdot Q$ for $1 \le k \le \ell/2$. Suppose given an additive homomorphism $\varphi$ from the points of $E$ to the points of the Vélu quotient `E.veluQuotient S` (the curve with the same $a_1, a_2, a_3$ and with $a_4 - 5\,\mathtt{veluTSum}\,S$, $a_6 - b_2\,\mathtt{veluTSum}\,S - 7\,\mathtt{veluWSum}\,S$) whose kernel is the subgroup $\mathbb{Z}Q$ of integer multiples of $Q$, and which on every affine point $(x,y)$ outside $\mathbb{Z}Q$ is given by $\varphi(x,y) = (\mathtt{veluX}\,S\,x,\ \mathtt{veluY}\,S\,x\,y)$ (for some witness of nonsingularity). The conclusion is an equality of pairs in the sigma type of a Weierstrass curve over $L$ together with a subgroup of its points: the pair $(E.\mathtt{stepCurve}\,H\,\ell,\ E.\mathtt{stepSubgroup}\,H\,\ell)$ equals $(E.\mathtt{veluQuotient}\,S,\ \varphi(H))$. Here, since $\ell \neq 2$, the first curve is `E.xVeluCurve` formed from the set of abscissae of the nonzero points $P \in H$ with $\ell \cdot P = 0$ (same $a_1,a_2,a_3$, and $a_4, a_6$ corrected by the `absSum` sums of `xVeluT` and `xVeluW` over that set), and the first subgroup is generated by the nonzero points of that curve whose abscissa lies in the image, under `E.xVeluX` for the same abscissa set, of the set of abscissae of the points $P \in H$ with $\ell \cdot P \neq 0$.
--
--   This identifies the odd prime-degree step of the iterated abscissa-indexed Vélu construction at a pair (curve, cyclic subgroup) with Vélu's quotient by the subgroup generated by a point of order $\ell$, the accompanying subgroup being the image of $H$ under the Vélu isogeny. It is used in the study of the $j$-invariants of cyclic quotients, for instance in the comparison of `cyclicQuotientJ` with roots of modular polynomials and with Tate-curve expansions.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_WeierstrassCurve_stepCurve_stepSubgroup_eq_of_prime_ne_two.lean

